1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,391
This one line is the most useful sentence anyone's going to say about your mission this week.

2
00:00:08,291 --> 00:00:11,770
It tells me which file and which line,

3
00:00:12,220 --> 00:00:17,002
what's wrong there, and what the game will do about it.

4
00:00:18,802 --> 00:00:21,846
So today you'll learn to read it,

5
00:00:22,296 --> 00:00:27,948
and we'll do that by breaking your mission in five places, on purpose.

6
00:00:29,748 --> 00:00:35,400
Lint is a program that reads your files and lists whatever looks wrong,

7
00:00:35,850 --> 00:00:37,589
and it changes nothing.

8
00:00:38,489 --> 00:00:41,533
Think of the editor at a publisher:

9
00:00:41,983 --> 00:00:44,157
she reads, and she marks,

10
00:00:44,607 --> 00:00:48,085
and you're the one who does the fixing.

11
00:00:49,885 --> 00:00:52,059
The habit has four beats.

12
00:00:52,959 --> 00:00:55,567
You change something, and you save.

13
00:00:56,017 --> 00:00:57,757
Then you run lint,

14
00:00:58,207 --> 00:01:01,685
and you read the first line it prints.

15
00:01:03,485 --> 00:01:06,528
This is the answer you're hoping for.

16
00:01:07,428 --> 00:01:10,472
There's the file, then the word clean,

17
00:01:10,922 --> 00:01:16,574
and then the count line, which says one fact sheet, one story file,

18
00:01:17,024 --> 00:01:19,198
no errors, and no warnings.

19
00:01:20,998 --> 00:01:23,607
Read that count line every time,

20
00:01:24,057 --> 00:01:26,230
even when it says clean.

21
00:01:28,030 --> 00:01:30,639
I'm going to misspell one field,

22
00:01:31,089 --> 00:01:33,263
then save, and run lint.

23
00:01:34,163 --> 00:01:36,337
And I get one warning.

24
00:01:38,137 --> 00:01:40,311
Every finding has four parts.

25
00:01:41,211 --> 00:01:44,689
First there's the level, which here is warning.

26
00:01:45,589 --> 00:01:47,328
Then there's the place,

27
00:01:47,778 --> 00:01:50,387
which is line twenty-eight, character one.

28
00:01:51,287 --> 00:01:53,461
Then comes the sentence itself.

29
00:01:54,361 --> 00:01:58,709
And at the very end, in brackets, there's the code,

30
00:01:59,159 --> 00:02:03,507
which is lint's short name for this kind of mistake.

31
00:02:05,307 --> 00:02:09,654
Now read the sentence all the way to the end,

32
00:02:10,104 --> 00:02:12,713
because it tells you three things.

33
00:02:13,613 --> 00:02:18,830
First, what's wrong: Done wen is not a field a quest has.

34
00:02:19,730 --> 00:02:24,513
Next, what the game does about it: nothing reads this line.

35
00:02:25,413 --> 00:02:30,196
And last, what to write instead: did you mean Done when.

36
00:02:31,996 --> 00:02:34,604
So I fix it, I save,

37
00:02:35,054 --> 00:02:38,098
I run lint again, and it's clean.

38
00:02:39,898 --> 00:02:42,507
Now, that was only a warning,

39
00:02:42,957 --> 00:02:46,000
so I could have played this mission.

40
00:02:46,900 --> 00:02:49,509
But with that one word misspelled,

41
00:02:49,959 --> 00:02:52,133
this step can never finish.

42
00:02:53,033 --> 00:02:58,250
A warning can stop your story just as surely as an error.

43
00:03:00,050 --> 00:03:02,659
Every writer does this next one.

44
00:03:03,559 --> 00:03:07,037
I wrote my line in a word processor,

45
00:03:07,487 --> 00:03:10,965
and it quietly made my quote marks curly.

46
00:03:11,865 --> 00:03:14,474
So now I get three warnings,

47
00:03:14,924 --> 00:03:16,663
one for each mark.

48
00:03:18,463 --> 00:03:21,072
All three are on line thirty-one,

49
00:03:21,522 --> 00:03:27,174
and this second number tells me how far along the line to look.

50
00:03:28,074 --> 00:03:30,248
Read to the end again:

51
00:03:30,698 --> 00:03:33,307
the game can't draw this mark,

52
00:03:33,757 --> 00:03:37,670
so it shows the plain one in its place.

53
00:03:38,570 --> 00:03:42,483
That means the crew would still read my sentence.

54
00:03:44,283 --> 00:03:46,891
It's a warning all the same,

55
00:03:47,341 --> 00:03:52,124
because the game only has a plain twin for some characters,

56
00:03:52,574 --> 00:03:56,487
and an emoji, for instance, is simply left out.

57
00:03:57,387 --> 00:04:01,735
So I delete each mark and press the plain key,

58
00:04:02,185 --> 00:04:03,924
and it's clean again.

59
00:04:05,724 --> 00:04:09,637
This time I rename a key, on line thirty-three.

60
00:04:10,537 --> 00:04:14,015
Lint complains about line twenty-nine and line thirty-six,

61
00:04:14,465 --> 00:04:17,943
and it says nothing at all about thirty-three.

62
00:04:18,843 --> 00:04:20,583
So why is that?

63
00:04:22,383 --> 00:04:25,426
Line twenty-nine points at the old key,

64
00:04:25,876 --> 00:04:29,354
and no record has that key any more,

65
00:04:29,804 --> 00:04:31,543
so nothing is revealed.

66
00:04:32,443 --> 00:04:35,922
And line thirty-six is the step I renamed.

67
00:04:36,372 --> 00:04:38,546
It's waiting to be revealed,

68
00:04:38,996 --> 00:04:41,170
and now nothing reveals it.

69
00:04:42,970 --> 00:04:46,883
Those are the two ends of one broken link,

70
00:04:47,333 --> 00:04:52,550
and lint names the line that points, and the line that waits.

71
00:04:54,350 --> 00:04:57,393
There's a second way to ask, too.

72
00:04:58,293 --> 00:05:03,076
If I run lint with the word missing on the end,

73
00:05:03,526 --> 00:05:09,613
I get a list of every key I've pointed at and haven't written yet.

74
00:05:10,513 --> 00:05:13,557
While I'm drafting, that's my to-do list.

75
00:05:15,357 --> 00:05:17,965
So I put the key back,

76
00:05:18,415 --> 00:05:21,893
and both warnings go away, and it's clean.

77
00:05:23,693 --> 00:05:26,302
Now I delete just one line,

78
00:05:26,752 --> 00:05:29,796
the three hyphens that close this fence.

79
00:05:30,696 --> 00:05:33,739
And that gets me my first error.

80
00:05:35,539 --> 00:05:37,278
I deleted line fifty-one,

81
00:05:37,728 --> 00:05:39,902
but lint names line forty-eight.

82
00:05:40,802 --> 00:05:43,846
That's the line where the fence opens,

83
00:05:44,296 --> 00:05:46,904
the fence that now never closes.

84
00:05:47,804 --> 00:05:50,848
And the sentence tells me the rest:

85
00:05:51,298 --> 00:05:55,211
the lines under the fields were read as fields,

86
00:05:55,661 --> 00:05:58,270
so this record has no text.

87
00:05:59,170 --> 00:06:02,213
It gives me the fix as well,

88
00:06:02,663 --> 00:06:08,315
which is to add a line of three hyphens under the last field.

89
00:06:10,115 --> 00:06:13,593
So I type it back, and it's clean.

90
00:06:14,493 --> 00:06:17,972
It's the same lesson as the last break:

91
00:06:18,422 --> 00:06:22,770
the line lint names isn't always the line I changed,

92
00:06:23,220 --> 00:06:27,133
so let the sentence tell you where to look.

93
00:06:28,933 --> 00:06:31,976
The hashes say where a record sits.

94
00:06:32,876 --> 00:06:36,789
So let's give this one a hash too many,

95
00:06:37,239 --> 00:06:38,978
and that's one error.

96
00:06:39,878 --> 00:06:41,617
This heading has four,

97
00:06:42,067 --> 00:06:45,546
and the one it sits under has two.

98
00:06:46,446 --> 00:06:50,359
And look at what the game does about it:

99
00:06:50,809 --> 00:06:54,722
it reads the heading as if it had three.

100
00:06:55,622 --> 00:06:58,665
Here that guess happens to be right,

101
00:06:59,115 --> 00:07:00,854
so nothing is lost.

102
00:07:02,654 --> 00:07:06,133
In break one, a warning stopped my story,

103
00:07:06,583 --> 00:07:09,626
and here an error costs me nothing.

104
00:07:10,526 --> 00:07:14,439
So I don't judge a finding by its level.

105
00:07:14,889 --> 00:07:16,628
I read its sentence.

106
00:07:18,428 --> 00:07:21,037
I take the extra hash off,

107
00:07:21,487 --> 00:07:26,270
and now let's go the other way, down to one hash.

108
00:07:27,170 --> 00:07:29,778
And that gives me six findings.

109
00:07:31,578 --> 00:07:35,057
This is the point where people give up,

110
00:07:35,507 --> 00:07:37,246
so here's the rule:

111
00:07:37,696 --> 00:07:39,870
read the first one only.

112
00:07:40,770 --> 00:07:44,248
It's about line forty-seven, the line I changed,

113
00:07:44,698 --> 00:07:50,350
and it says one hash starts a new title, so give it three.

114
00:07:51,250 --> 00:07:54,728
The others are about two records further down,

115
00:07:55,178 --> 00:07:56,917
records I never touched,

116
00:07:57,367 --> 00:08:00,846
and their advice would actually make things worse.

117
00:08:02,646 --> 00:08:05,254
So I put the hashes back,

118
00:08:05,704 --> 00:08:08,313
and all six go at once.

119
00:08:09,213 --> 00:08:13,126
Fix the first finding, and then run lint again.

120
00:08:14,926 --> 00:08:17,100
Now for the honest part.

121
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:20,174
I leave myself a note,

122
00:08:20,624 --> 00:08:26,711
the way I would in a manuscript, with a hash in front of it.

123
00:08:27,611 --> 00:08:29,350
And lint says clean.

124
00:08:30,250 --> 00:08:36,337
But in the game, my note is part of the description the crew reads.

125
00:08:38,137 --> 00:08:42,050
In this file, a note starts with two slashes.

126
00:08:43,850 --> 00:08:47,763
Clean means every shape is one that lint knows,

127
00:08:48,213 --> 00:08:50,822
every key that's pointed at exists,

128
00:08:51,272 --> 00:08:54,315
and the story file can be read.

129
00:08:55,215 --> 00:08:59,998
It doesn't mean the mission does what I meant it to.

130
00:09:00,898 --> 00:09:06,115
The page has a short list of the things lint can't see.

131
00:09:07,915 --> 00:09:09,654
Lint reads your files,

132
00:09:10,104 --> 00:09:12,278
but it doesn't run them.

133
00:09:13,178 --> 00:09:17,526
So the second check is to play for a minute,

134
00:09:17,976 --> 00:09:20,150
and then read the logs.

135
00:09:21,950 --> 00:09:26,298
Here's that extra hash again, this time in the game.

136
00:09:27,198 --> 00:09:29,372
The quest list looks fine,

137
00:09:29,822 --> 00:09:33,735
because the game took the hash off for me.

138
00:09:34,635 --> 00:09:36,374
And here's the log:

139
00:09:36,824 --> 00:09:40,737
one line, with the line number and the fix.

140
00:09:41,637 --> 00:09:45,550
That's exactly what lint told me before I played,

141
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:48,609
which is why lint comes first.

142
00:09:50,409 --> 00:09:53,887
So I fix it, and lint says clean.

143
00:09:54,787 --> 00:09:58,700
But the log still has its line in it,

144
00:09:59,150 --> 00:10:01,759
because lint leaves the logs alone.

145
00:10:02,659 --> 00:10:06,137
They're the game's notes on my last play.

146
00:10:07,937 --> 00:10:11,415
So I play again, and there's my story.

147
00:10:12,315 --> 00:10:14,924
This time both logs are empty,

148
00:10:15,374 --> 00:10:19,722
and that's what you want to see after every play.

149
00:10:21,522 --> 00:10:24,565
Now use it on your own words.

150
00:10:25,465 --> 00:10:28,943
Rewrite the three descriptions, and the three readings,

151
00:10:29,393 --> 00:10:35,480
and after every one of them, save, run lint, and read the count line.

152
00:10:36,380 --> 00:10:39,859
Then play the mission, and read both logs.

153
00:10:41,659 --> 00:10:47,746
Next time, we'll put names to two words you saw in this file today:

154
00:10:48,196 --> 00:10:50,370
a role, and a signal.
